<reading-questions xml:id="reflections-det">
<title>Reflect on your understanding</title>

<introduction>

	<p> The reflections activities in this section concern only <em>square</em> matrices. </p>

	<p>
	In addition to the reflection activities below,
	re-read <xref ref="section-det-terminology" text="type-global-title" />.
	Be sure you understand each of the new definitions introduced in this chapter,
	and spend some time committing them to memory.
	</p>

</introduction>

<exercise>
	<title><m>1 \times 1</m> determinants</title>
	<p>
	Explain the conceptual reason we have defined the determinant of the general <m>1 \times 1</m> matrix <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}</m> to be the value of the single entry <m>a</m>.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Inductive definition of the determinant</title>
	<p>
	Describe how the definition of the determinant for an <m>n \times n</m> matrix depends on the determinant for <m>(n - 1) \times (n - 1)</m> matrices
	(assuming <m>n \ge 2</m>).
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Minors versus cofactors</title>
	<p> Describe the relationship between the <m>\nth[(i,j)]</m> minor and the <m>\nth[(i,j)]</m> cofactor of a matrix for a specific pair of indices <m>(i,j)</m>. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Minors/cofactors versus entries</title>

	<task><p>
		State which entries in an <m>n \times n</m> matrix are involved in the computation of the <m>\nth[(i,j)]</m> minor of that matrix.
		Repeat for the computation of the <m>\nth[(i,j)]</m> cofactor.
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		In particular,
		do the values of the <m>\nth[(i,j)]</m> minor and cofactor of a matrix depend on the value of the <m>\nth[(i,j)]</m> entry of that matrix?
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Cofactor signs</title>
	<p> Describe in words the general pattern of cofactor signs in an <m>n \times n</m> matrix. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Determinants of diagonal/triangular matrices</title>
	<task><p> State the simple pattern for computing the determinant of a diagonal or upper/lower triangular matrix. </p></task>
	<task><p> What conclusion(s) can be made about a diagonal or triangular matrix whose determinant is known to be zero? </p></task>
</exercise>

</reading-questions>
